
  [1mThe fork:[0m there is no [38;5;153muserId[39m anywhere in this codebase, so the question isn't how to add the parameter — it's where
  the caller gets a value to pass. Each answer commits you to a different interface.

  - [1mNew form field[0m — user types their ID. Buys: a real, user-supplied ID with no plumbing. Costs: an HTML change and a
    [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m rule; asks users for something they usually don't know. Rules out treating userId as server-assigned
    later without a migration.
  - [1mClient-generated correlation ID[0m — [38;5;153mlogin()[39m gets a caller-supplied ID (crypto.randomUUID or similar) purely to tie
    events together. Buys: works today with no backend or form change. Costs: it identifies a [3msession[0m, not a person —
    "who logged in" is still just the username. Cheap to change later.
  - [1mRead from existing client state[0m — pull from localStorage/a cookie set by a previous session. Buys: a stable
    per-browser identity. Costs: absent on first login, so the parameter is optional and often undefined. Moderately
    cheap to change.
  - [1mNot a parameter at all[0m — the server returns the userId in the login response and you log it there. Buys: the
    actually-authoritative ID, since identity is what login [3mestablishes[0m. Costs: doesn't match your literal request, and
    the API call is currently a stub, so nothing returns a real ID yet.

  [1mMy recommendation: the fourth.[0m A login function's job is to determine who the user is; an ID passed [3min[0m by the caller
  can't be trusted as the answer to "who logged in," and here the caller has nothing to pass. That said, the stub
  returns [38;5;153m{ success: true, user: username }[39m with no network call, so this option is design-only until the real POST
  exists — which is why the correlation ID is the best option if you want something working today.
